# Atlas DSL Cookbook (single-file, runnable)
#
# This file is intentionally long and heavily commented.
# It is meant to be a "try everything" playground you can run end-to-end:
#
#   atlas run examples/atlas_cookbook.atl
#
# or on Windows:
#
#   python -m atlas run examples\atlas_cookbook.atl
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0) What Atlas is (and what it is not)
#
# Atlas is a small, line-oriented DSL designed for *risk scripting* on CSV data.
# The focus is:
# - fast iteration
# - clear, inspectable intermediate tables
# - familiar quant workflows (returns, rolling vol, VaR/ES, corr/cov, beta, Sharpe)
#
# It is not (yet) a full research platform:
# - no portfolio optimizer
# - no full-blown backtesting engine
# - no factor model library
#
# The goal of this cookbook is to give you a "hands-on map" of the core verbs and
# a few higher-signal diagnostics.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1) Load prices and compute returns
#
# `load` reads CSV via pandas. Relative paths are resolved from this file’s
# directory (`examples/`), so `prices.csv` works.
#
prices = load "prices.csv"
close = select prices column=close
rets_simple = returns close simple
rets_log = returns close log
#
# Print objects at any time:
print rets_simple
print rets_log
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2) Rolling volatility
#
# The default is a sample standard deviation on the rolling window.
# Use the positional window form or `window=`.
#
vol21 = vol rets_log 21
vol63 = vol rets_log window=63
print vol21
print vol63
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3) VaR and ES (historical vs parametric Gaussian)
#
# Convention:
# - `level=0.95` means 95% confidence => left-tail mass is 5%
# - VaR is a left-tail quantile of returns
# - ES is the mean of returns in the tail (worse than VaR threshold)
#
var95_hist = var rets_log level=0.95 method=historical
es95_hist = es rets_log level=0.95 method=historical
var99_hist = var rets_log level=0.99 method=historical
es99_hist = es rets_log level=0.99 method=historical
#
var95_norm = var rets_log level=0.95 method=parametric
es95_norm = es rets_log level=0.95 method=parametric
var99_norm = var rets_log level=0.99 method=parametric
es99_norm = es rets_log level=0.99 method=parametric
#
print var95_hist
print es95_hist
print var99_hist
print es99_hist
print var95_norm
print es95_norm
print var99_norm
print es99_norm
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4) Distribution diagnostics (summary + Jarque–Bera)
#
# `summary` gives quick stats you normally eyeball before risk metrics:
# - scale (mean/std)
# - tail quantiles (p05/p95)
# - skew / kurtosis
#
# `jb` is Jarque–Bera normality test (useful to remind yourself why
# parametric Gaussian risk can be optimistic under fat tails).
#
sum_log = summary rets_log
jb_log = jb rets_log
print sum_log
print jb_log
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5) Drawdown and max drawdown (from returns)
#
# `drawdown` is computed from cumulative wealth:
#   wealth_t = Π (1 + r_t)
#   drawdown_t = wealth_t / max_to_date(wealth) - 1
#
# `maxdd` returns the most negative drawdown in the period.
#
dd = drawdown rets_log
mxdd = maxdd rets_log
print mxdd
print dd
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6) Multi-asset workflows: bind, corr, cov, beta
#
# Use `prices_wide.csv` which contains two synthetic "close" series.
# The typical workflow is:
# - select single columns
# - compute returns per asset
# - `bind` to form a wide return matrix
# - run `cov` / `corr`
#
wide_prices = load "prices_wide.csv"
close_a = select wide_prices column=close_a
close_b = select wide_prices column=close_b
ra = returns close_a log
rb = returns close_b log
R = bind ra rb
#
cm = cov R
cr = corr R
print cm
print cr
#
# `beta` expects single-column frames. Here we treat `rb` as "market".
#
bet_a_vs_b = beta ra mkt=rb
print bet_a_vs_b
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7) Sharpe ratio
#
# Annualized Sharpe on returns. Use `periods=` to match your sampling.
# Daily returns => 252, weekly => 52, monthly => 12.
#
sh = sharpe rets_log periods=252
print sh
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8) Simple "portfolio" return by linear combination
#
# `lincomb` takes a wide return matrix and weights, returning a single series.
# This is a minimal building block for portfolio risk *without* adding new syntax.
#
port = lincomb R weights=0.6,0.4
print port
#
# Then compute risk and a basic exceedance diagnostic.
#
v_port = var port level=0.95 method=historical
bt_port = var_backtest port var=v_port level=0.95
print v_port
print bt_port
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9) Notes on interpretation and next steps
#
# - If you get surprising VaR signs: remember VaR here is a return quantile.
#   For log returns, you expect negative VaR for losses at typical levels.
# - If you want "positive loss" conventions, you can transform downstream
#   (e.g., multiply by -1) — that can be added as a new command later if needed.
#
# Recommended habits when scripting risk:
# - Always print `summary` for the return series you use.
# - Compare historical vs parametric. If they diverge materially, inspect tails.
# - For multi-asset workflows, check both `corr` and `cov` and ensure data alignment.
# - Use drawdown diagnostics for sanity even if your risk metric is VaR/ES.
#
# End of cookbook.

